###Strategy

We can find $n\%$ of a number like this:

$\begin{align}
\goldE{n}\% \text{ of }(\maroonD{\text{whole}}) &= \blueE{\text{part}}
\\\\
\dfrac{\goldE{n}}{100} \cdot \maroonD{\text{whole}} &= \blueE{\text{part}}
\end{align}$

We know that the tax is $8\%$. If we identify the part and the whole, we can write a general equation that relates how much tax $(t)$ Marta pays on an item whose price is $p$ dollars.

Then, we can find how much tax Marta pays on an item whose price is $\$ 20$.

###Writing an equation

Our $\maroonD{\text{whole}}$ is the item's price $\maroonD{p}$, and the $\blueE{\text{part}}$ is how much money Marta pays $(\blueE{t})$ with the $\goldE{8}\%$ tax:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\goldE{n}}{100} \cdot \maroonD{\text{whole}} &= \blueE{\text{part}}
\\\\
\dfrac{\goldE{8}}{100} \cdot \maroonD{p} &= \blueE{t}
\\\\
0.08 \maroonD{p} &= \blueE{t}
\\\\
\blueE{t} &= 0.08 \maroonD{p}
\end{align}$
